 "result": "Order of calls, from the repo's `brain/request-lifecycle.md` note (I didn't open the source). All of these are in `src/tessel/app.py` unless a path is given.\n\n1. **`Tessel.__call__` → `Tessel.wsgi_entry(environ, start_response)`** (~L1566).\n2. **`Tessel.request_context(environ)` → `AppContext.from_environ`** (`src/tessel/ctx.py`).\n   - Builds `request_class(environ)` and sets `request.json_module`.\n   - Calls `app.build_url_binder(request)`, which makes the bound `MapAdapter`. A failure is stored as `routing_exception`.\n3. **`AppContext.push()`** (`src/tessel/ctx.py`).\n   - Sends `appcontext_pushed`.\n   - Opens the session via `_get_session`.\n   - Calls `match_request()`, which sets `request.url_rule` and `request.view_args`, or `request.routing_exception`.\n4. **`run_full_request(ctx)`**:\n   - Sends `request_started`.\n   - **`preprocess_request`** runs the `url_value_preprocessor`s, then the `before_request` functions. A non-None return short-circuits the view.\n   - **`run_view`** raises any routing exception, handles automatic OPTIONS via `make_default_options_response`, and otherwise calls `ensure_sync(view_functions[rule.endpoint])(**view_args)`.\n\nIf the view or a hook raises, `route_user_fault` → `route_http_fault` or a registered handler takes over. The handler is found by `_pick_fault_handler` in `src/tessel/sansio/app.py`.",
